def install(exact):
    entries = '\nthe pool tables|Felt-topped billiards tables divide the recreation floor. Clear space surrounds each table, with a watching bench set back against the room\'s edge.\nthe kitchen displays|Staged kitchen arrangements divide the furniture showroom into demonstration bays. A browsing aisle separates the display counters and cabinets; their careful arrangement presents an example interior within the larger shop.\nthe bedroom displays|Coordinated bed frames and bedroom furnishings occupy separate showroom arrangements. A browsing aisle gives visitors space to compare the displays without entering their close-set furniture groupings.\nthe living room displays|Sofas and low tables form staged sitting rooms across the showroom. A browsing aisle passes between the display groupings, emphasizing their different proportions and finishes.\nthe dining room displays|Matched tables and chairs occupy separate display settings. A browsing aisle leaves room to compare their sizes and finishes across the furniture showroom.\nthe office displays|Desks and office chairs form compact demonstration settings. A browsing aisle separates the staged workspaces, whose careful arrangements contrast with the open shop floor.\nthe outdoor displays|Weather-resistant furniture stands in small display groups. A browsing aisle lies between the arrangements, giving the retail space the organized character of an outdoor-living showroom.\nthe Fire Temple of Yazd, Iran|Pale masonry gives the temple a restrained, weighty presence. The ceremonial space lies clear within the surrounding structure, with carefully finished surfaces emphasizing the room\'s ceremonial purpose.\nSalerno Cathedral|Substantial masonry and long architectural lines give the cathedral interior a solemn scale. A prayer bench faces the central observance space, where the open floor contrasts with the weight of the surrounding structure.\nthe Temple of Sin beneath the Colonial at Tranquility Farm|Dark devotional ornament interrupts the heavy surfaces of the subterranean chamber. A ceremonial space lies beneath the low structural mass, giving the temple a private, deliberately unsettling character.\nthe Supernatural History classroom|Historical display panels surround the classroom\'s teaching area, their dense arrangements contrasting with the familiar student desks. Fine scratches cloud the desktops. From the front desk, the lesson space lies plainly in view, bringing the study of extraordinary histories down to the ordinary scale of a classroom seat.\nthe Abnormal Psychology classroom|Plain desks face a teaching area arranged for discussion. The restrained, almost clinical finishes leave little visual distraction from the lesson, though older scratches survive on the desktops. The front desk brings students close to the presentation space, where even an ordinary question has few corners in which to hide.\nthe Latin classroom|Student desks face a teaching surface bordered by text-heavy instructional panels. The front desk sits near the lesson space, where the room\'s spare arrangement encourages close attention to language.\nthe Latin room|Text-heavy instructional panels border a compact teaching space. Student desks occupy the center, with the front desk close to the plain presentation surface.\nthe forensics classroom|Washable demonstration surfaces face rows of student desks. Small abrasions mark the places cleaned most often, and the room carries a faintly sharp institutional smell. The front desk overlooks the practical teaching area at close range, with little clutter between the students and whatever detail the lesson asks them to notice.\nforensics storage|Separated compartments line the storage room, their regular edges broken only by small handling marks. The dry air smells of packaging and repeatedly cleaned surfaces. A cataloguing table offers a clear station among the ranks, with room to examine material carefully before giving it a place in the surrounding order.\nthe emergency room|Washable partitions divide the emergency room into spare treatment spaces. Repeated cleaning has dulled the surfaces nearest the working areas, and antiseptic lends the air a sharp edge. A waiting bench occupies the public margin, close enough to watch the clear floor while remaining outside the practical business beyond it.\nthe recovery wing|Pale finishes and an open floor give the recovery wing a measured quiet. The fittings bear faint marks of cleaning, and the air carries a restrained medicinal smell. A waiting bench sits along the margin, offering visitors a place to settle without turning the room\'s calm into a gathering in the middle.\nthe diagnosis wing|Clean work surfaces and plain partitions divide the diagnostic interior. A consultation table provides a place for quiet discussion within the room\'s measured clinical layout.\nthe intervention wing|Washable surfaces and spare fittings give the intervention area a controlled, practical shape. A consultation table sits clear of the central floor, with little decorative clutter in the surrounding space.\nthe experimentation wing|Repeated laboratory surfaces border a closely controlled institutional interior. A workbench provides a defined station beside the clear central floor, the worn marks standing out against the room\'s otherwise restrained finish.\nthe psychiatrists office|A desk stands apart from a small consultation setting, giving the office two different kinds of conversation. Subdued finishes and a trace of furniture polish soften the clinical surroundings. The visitor chair faces the working surface, where even the clear desktop seems to leave deliberate room for whatever has brought someone here.\nthe headmaster\'s office|A substantial desk faces a precisely placed visitor chair. Fine woodwork and an uncluttered floor give the headmaster\'s office a dry, formal gravity. Wear shows most clearly at the working edge of the desk, where ordinary hands have softened the finish of a room otherwise arranged to make a visitor sit up straight.\nthe Master of the Clinic\'s Office of the White Oak Institute|A broad desk divides White Oak\'s pale, severe office. The visitor chair faces it across a carefully kept gap, leaving little opportunity for a meeting to feel casual. Even the storage has a clinical neatness. Small marks on the desktop stand out like imperfections under an examining light.\nthe Spender Center computer lab|Rows of computer stations divide the lab into repeated workspaces. Cable channels and durable flooring give the room a practical institutional finish, with a shared workstation providing a place for close collaboration.\nthe biodome laboratory|Washable work surfaces occupy a controlled research space within the biodome structure. Planted-environment textures give way to the plain workbench and clear floor, emphasizing the distinction between cultivation and examination.\nthe grain silo|Curving agricultural walls rise around a narrow central space. Fine grain dust settles in the structural seams, accentuating the tall, enclosed geometry of the storage chamber.\nThirsty Mind Coffee and Wine Bar|Coffee and the faint fruity trace of old wine mingle around Thirsty Mind\'s service counter. Small tables gather beneath warm finishes, their surfaces marked by the ghosts of earlier glasses. A corner table sits just outside the busiest arrangement, offering a comfortable place for a conversation to outlast the first drink.\nthe cozy interior of Boston Beach \'n BBQ|The room holds a settled smell of wood and old barbecue smoke. Sturdy dining furniture crowds the worn floor, with a shared table at the center and the marks of many meals along the edges. Coastal touches soften the robust fittings, giving the interior an easy, elbows-on-the-table warmth.\nMamma Maria, Italian Restaurant|Warm woodwork frames carefully separated dining settings at Mamma Maria. The air carries a faint, lingering trace of herbs beneath the clean table surfaces. A corner table rests just beyond the main arrangements, where close chairs and subdued finishes bring dinner down to the comfortable scale of an unhurried conversation.\nRockin\' Pub|Music-themed decoration crowds the pub\'s dark woodwork. The bar counter wears a dull shine beneath old drink rings, and the air carries a settled trace of beer and furniture polish. A watching bench stands back from the open middle, giving company a place to take in the room between rounds of conversation.\nThe SalteBox Cafe|Salt-weathered decorative wood gives the SalteBox the faded colors of things left near the sea. Small dining settings gather before the service counter, with scuffs under the chairs and a faint coffee smell in the close air. A corner table rests at the edge, comfortably tucked into the cafe\'s unpretentious coastal clutter.\nMandarin Oriental Hotel, Boston - Residential Suite|Carefully finished wood and soft textiles define a spacious hotel interior. A sofa stands apart from the quieter sleeping furniture, giving the suite distinct places for company and rest.\nthe Pyramids View Inn - Lobby|Pale plaster and warm wood trim frame a modest reception interior. A waiting bench sits along one wall, leaving the center clear for arrivals within the inn\'s compact public space.\nMangonui Fish Shop|Washable surfaces and sturdy retail fittings give the fish shop a practical waterfront character. A service counter occupies the customer edge, with clear flooring worn smooth where people wait.\nMangonui Fish Shop Cafe Seating Area|Simple tables and hard-wearing chairs form a casual dining space. A corner table sits at the edge, where the practical finishes retain the unpretentious character of the fish shop.\ndingy warehouse retrofitted into a rave|Exposed beams span the broad dance floor, their old industrial scars still visible above the club fittings. Heel marks cross older handling stains on the ground. A watching bench stands against the rough warehouse walls, where the fabric carries the mingled traces of perfume and the building\'s stubborn concrete dust.\na Longliner Fishing Boat named "Filthy Oar"|The longliner\'s work-worn deck lies within a close framework of marine fittings. Salt-marked surfaces and repeated joints give the Filthy Oar a practical shape, with a open floor kept clear among the fixed structures.\nPublic Garden and Boston Common|Cultivated grass and mature planting give this public ground a broad, carefully tended texture. Worn patches record ordinary use, while the planted edges lend the open gathering space a softer boundary.\nthe Dark Workshop of the Artisan|Fine tool marks cover the Artisan\'s darkened workbench, crossing older scars in patient, deliberate lines. Dust has settled into the deepest cuts. The surrounding stations keep a careful order, drawing attention toward the small working surfaces where something rough might, given enough time, become something exact.\nViTech Corp- Lobby|Crisp corporate finishes frame ViTech\'s reception space. The broad floor reflects the careful attention given to appearances, though a few scuffs survive along the busiest route. A waiting bench stands against the wall, offering visitors a precisely placed pause inside the company\'s polished, impersonal welcome.\nVitech Corp- Basement Lab|The building\'s heavy lower structure closes around ViTech\'s washable laboratory surfaces. Small abrasions dull the workbench beneath a faint trace of cleaning chemicals. The room has a compact, contained precision, with the plain walls bringing every station into uncomfortable proximity with the others.\na dim-lit Spa and Massage Parlor named \'Deep Dark Zen-tasies\'|Dark finishes and soft dividers break the parlor into intimate treatment spaces. A chaise provides a quiet place near the edge, where the interior\'s deliberate softness meets the more work-worn surfaces.\na Shady, Smoky Bar called \'Don Santiago Saloon\'|Dark wood and heavily used furnishings give the saloon a close, worn character. A bar counter faces the standing floor, with a corner table occupying a less exposed position against the wall.\nthe dubiously decadent Lobby of Hotel Paradise|Glossy decorative surfaces compete with scuffed practical fittings in the hotel lobby. A waiting bench sits beneath the elaborate trim, leaving the arrival space open in the middle.\na dark Underground Club known as The Crimson Devil|Crimson accents cut through the club\'s dark finishes beneath the weight of the surrounding structure. The dance floor bears a crosshatching of heels and dragged soles. A watching bench sits back along the edge, where upholstery catches a trace of perfume and old drinks in the close underground air.\nthe Sami museum of Siida|Carefully separated exhibits occupy a restrained gallery. A viewing area gives visitors room to examine the displays, with simple finishes leaving the exhibited material as the room\'s focus.\n'
